
Reflectivity/Emissivity Files in MODTRAN

     The data for the spectral emissivities and reflectivities for the
background are in a separate file (File NF09).  The data can be given at
arbitrary wavenumber or wavelength points; Subroutine RHOEPS interpolates
(linearly) these data onto the wavenumber grid of the calculation.  The
data sequence for the background reflectivity file is:


  CARD     FORMAT            DESCRIPTION
    1      9A4             Title card - ignored
    2       I2             JFILE - Number of backgrounds
    3     13X,I2           LBKG - Background number
    4      I1              JUNIT - (0,1) for (microns,wavenumber)
    5   I1,F9.0,2x,2F4.2   JDUM,VVIN,(R,E)
                             JDUM = Dummy
                             VVIN = Wavenumber (cm-1)/micron (um)
                             R    = Reflectivity
                             E    = Emissivity
    6             Repeat Card 5 for Each VVIN
    7       I1             J - End of file when J = 9 (or any non-zero value)

  8-12            Repeat Cards 3-7 for Next Background Entry

   13       A4             END - End of data file (not used).


The end of each reflectivity-emissivity file is specified by a 9 in Column 1.
The total number of files to be read is given by JFILE in Card 2.  The end of
the data file is given by END, read under an A4 Format.  There are some
extra background files at the end (constant albedos) which were used for
debugging.  Fifty-two (52) data entries for each background are allowed.  If
this is not enough, change the value of KKMAX in subroutine RHOEPS.

     The background emissivity (E on Card 5) is not used, because MODTRAN is
assuming that it always equals (1 - SALB).  If a wavelength or wavenumber of
the calculation should be outside of the spectral range of this data file,
the nearest (first or last) entry is used.

     The actual values for these background files are undocumented!  They
represent some generic values I put together over ten years ago for a target
signature code and have modified as needed.  The cloud file should be used
with some care.  It has a reflectivity that is greater than 1.0, in order to
attempt to include some strong forward scatter for smaller scattering angles.
When used by MODTRAN for the surface albedo, I am not sure what happens---
things like TOTAL reflectivity greater than 1.0.  Fortunately negative
emissivities are not a problem becasuse there is a check in TRANS.

Dave Robertson, SSI
